#!/bin/sh
#
# DIR-842 R1 (RTL8197F) — enable netfilter SOFTWARE flow offloading.
#
# Measured on the bench (RTL8197F @ 1 GHz, single core), WAN->LAN NAT download,
# iperf3 -R over 10 s, CPU sampled from /proc/stat on the box:
#
#   without offload : 147 Mbit/s @ 96% CPU   (54.7 ms CPU per MB)
#   with offload    : 181 Mbit/s @ 90% CPU   (40.7 ms CPU per MB)
#
# +23% throughput and -25% CPU per byte, so it is a clear win and costs nothing.
#
# ★ Why this matters on THIS board specifically: the rtl865x ASIC offloads the
# FORWARD (LAN->WAN) direction only. The WAN->LAN reverse path has no hardware
# offload on this silicon — stock firmware traps every inbound packet too, which
# was verified by booting stock and measuring it (see
# docs/M7-HWNAT-REVERSE-NAPT.md). So every downloaded byte is forwarded by the
# CPU, and the only lever left on that direction is making the software path
# cheaper. This is that lever.
#
# NOT a fix for the remaining gap: the box is still CPU-bound at ~181 Mbit and
# gigabit needs roughly 8 ms CPU/MB, so ~5x more is still owed by the driver
# datapath (DMA cache maintenance + uncached descriptor reads dominate; the RX
# path is already zero-copy). Flow offload only removes the conntrack/iptables
# traversal, which was never the dominant cost here.
#
# fw3 appends "--hw" to the rule; harmless — this driver implements no flowtable
# ndo, so nf_flow_table_hw finds no hardware and it stays a software flowtable.
[ -n "$(uci -q get firewall.@defaults[0])" ] || exit 0
uci -q set firewall.@defaults[0].flow_offloading='1'
uci -q commit firewall
exit 0
